# Small, transparent starter corpus. Expand only with reviewed and attributable sources.
KNOWLEDGE = [
 {"id":"climate-01","title":"Climate change fundamentals","topic":"climate","text":"Climate change refers to long-term shifts in temperatures and weather patterns. Human activities, especially burning fossil fuels, increase heat-trapping greenhouse gas concentrations. Mitigation reduces emissions or increases removals; adaptation reduces harm from climate impacts.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; verify against IPCC assessments before production."},
 {"id":"energy-01","title":"Renewable electricity","topic":"energy","text":"Solar, wind and hydropower generate electricity from replenished energy flows. Their lifecycle impacts depend on materials, siting, grid integration, storage and end-of-life management. Comparing technologies requires a consistent lifecycle boundary and functional unit.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; verify against IEA and lifecycle assessment literature."},
 {"id":"biodiversity-01","title":"Biodiversity and ecosystems","topic":"biodiversity","text":"Biodiversity includes variation within species, between species and across ecosystems. Habitat conversion, overexploitation, invasive species, pollution and climate change are major pressures. Conservation combines protected areas, restoration and sustainable management with local and Indigenous knowledge.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; verify against IPBES assessments."},
 {"id":"waste-01","title":"Waste prevention and circularity","topic":"waste","text":"The waste hierarchy prioritizes prevention and reuse before recycling, recovery and disposal. Circular economy strategies design out waste, keep products and materials in use, and regenerate natural systems. Recycling performance depends on collection, sorting, material quality and local infrastructure.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; verify against UNEP resources."},
 {"id":"water-01","title":"Water stewardship","topic":"water","text":"Water stress depends on withdrawals relative to available renewable supplies and local ecological needs. Efficient irrigation, leakage reduction, watershed restoration and reuse can help, but interventions should be assessed at basin scale to avoid shifting scarcity elsewhere.","source":"EcoScholar editorial starter note; verify against UNESCO and UN-Water resources."},
]
